{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And the shepherds came and drove them away.</b> Since he was a priest and the great one of Midian, how could the shepherds drive his daughters away? Rather, the verse teaches you that they had ostracized him from among them, because he had removed from his house all utensils of idolatry before Moses came to him, and they agreed among themselves that they would do no work for him nor graze his flock. Therefore his daughters needed to graze his flock themselves, and the shepherds would drive them away." "<b>And Moses rose and saved them.</b> From the violence that the shepherds were doing to them — for surely, since the daughters drew the water and filled the troughs, the water was theirs; and the water in the troughs did not suffice for all the sheep, and therefore he watered their flock first."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:38.996307681Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}